# Q1: How would a human perform this motion? (One sentence)
A human raises both hands to shoulder/head height and repeatedly crooks and un-crooks their index and middle fingers to signify sarcasm or quotation.
# Q2: Pose Candidates Comparison. Rank at least 2 pose configurations (torso_height, arm_position, gripper_orientation, head, left_arm, x/y/z) and state the winner and why—do NOT pick presets from gesture names alone.
# Q2: Candidate 1: `arm_position: "up"`, `elbow_bended: true`, `left_arm: "mirror"`, `z: 75`. This places both hands symmetrically near the head, which is the classic air quotes stance. Candidate 2: `arm_position: "front"`, `elbow_bended: true`, `left_arm: "mirror"`, `z: 75`. This pushes the hands too far forward, away from the body. Winner: Candidate 1, as it is the most iconic and recognizable starting position.
# Q3: Structure Comparison. Compare 2+ full primitive sequences (e.g., M1: pose > movement > pose vs M2: pose > path > path). State the winner and why—prioritize movement/path beats that express the cue, not pose-only tweaks.
# Q3: Structure 1: `pose` -> `movement` on `wrist.z` (pronation/supination) with `repetition: 2`. This uses a wrist twist. Structure 2: `pose` -> `movement` on `wrist.x` (flexion/extension) with `repetition: 2`. This uses a wrist flick. Winner: Structure 2, because the up-and-down flicking motion (`wrist.x`) more closely emulates the human "crooking" of fingers than a simple twist does.
# Q4: Context Reinforcement. Compare 2+ micro-timing choices (speed, repetition, hold_time, torso/head expression) that make the gesture read stronger and pick the best one.
# Q4: Choice 1: Fast speed (`speed: 3.5`) and `repetition: 2`. This creates a quick, sharp, double-flick that matches the sarcastic and brisk tone of the gesture. Choice 2: Slower speed (`speed: 2.0`) and `repetition: 3`. This feels more deliberate and less snappy, losing the intended sarcastic quality. Winner: Choice 1, for its sharp timing which reinforces the cue's meaning.
